</history><permissions><!--Copyright &#x000a9; 2025 European Academy of Neurology--><copyright-statement content-type="article-copyright">&#x000a9; 2025 The Author(s). <italic toggle="yes">European Journal of Neurology</italic> published by John Wiley &#x00026; Sons Ltd on behalf of European Academy of Neurology.</copyright-statement><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an open access article under the terms of the <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ext-link> License, which permits use and distribution in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited, the use is non&#x02010;commercial and no modifications or adaptations are made.</license-p></license></permissions><self-uri content-type="pdf" xlink:href="file:ENE-32-e70077.pdf"/><abstract><title>ABSTRACT</title><sec id="ene70077-sec-0001"><title>Background</title><p>Parkinson's disease (PD) treatments, such as apomorphine (APO) and levodopa&#x02013;carbidopa intestinal gel (LCIG), represent advanced therapeutic options for managing motor symptoms. However, clear selection criteria and well&#x02010;defined cognitive outcomes are lacking. This systematic review specifically aimed to address these gaps by assessing the cognitive impact of APO and LCIG in PD patients.</p></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0002"><title>Methods</title><p>A systematic review was conducted following PRISMA guidelines, with searches in PubMed, Web of Science, Scopus, and Embase. Two authors screened studies based on key inclusion criteria, including at least two cognitive tests, and a follow&#x02010;up of 6&#x02009;months or more. The risk of bias was evaluated using the Newcastle&#x02013;Ottawa Scale (NOS).</p></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0003"><title>Results</title><p>Fifteen studies were identified (7 APO and 8 LCIG). APO generally preserved cognitive function over a 12&#x02010;month follow&#x02010;up, with some decreases in visuospatial memory and executive functions. LCIG, with a 28&#x02010;month follow&#x02010;up, showed more extensive cognitive decline, particularly in patients with pre&#x02010;existing impairments. Variability in cognitive tests made direct comparisons difficult.</p></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0004"><title>Discussion</title><p>APO may have a more favorable cognitive profile than LCIG. However, differences in follow&#x02010;up duration, moderate risk of bias, and inconsistent cognitive assessments warrant cautious interpretation. Improved patient selection and comprehensive cognitive evaluations are recommended for future practice.</p></sec></abstract><kwd-group kwd-group-type="author-generated"><kwd id="ene70077-kwd-0001">advanced therapies</kwd><kwd id="ene70077-kwd-0002">apomorphine</kwd><kwd id="ene70077-kwd-0003">cognition</kwd><kwd id="ene70077-kwd-0004">levodopa&#x02013;carbidopa intestinal gel</kwd><kwd id="ene70077-kwd-0005">Parkinson's disease</kwd></kwd-group><counts><fig-count count="1"/><table-count count="4"/><page-count count="16"/><word-count count="9200"/></counts><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>source-schema-version-number</meta-name><meta-value>2.0</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>cover-date</meta-name><meta-value>February 2025</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>details-of-publishers-convertor</meta-name><meta-value>Converter:WILEY_ML3GV2_TO_JATSPMC version:6.5.3 mode:remove_FC converted:11.02.2025</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta><notes><fn-group id="ene70077-ntgp-0001"><fn fn-type="funding" id="ene70077-note-0001"><p>
<bold>Funding:</bold> The authors received no specific funding for this work.</p></fn></fn-group></notes></front><body id="ene70077-body-0001"><sec id="ene70077-sec-0005"><label>1</label><title>Introduction</title><p>Parkinson's disease (PD) is a neurodegenerative disorder marked by the loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra. Besides motor symptoms, growing evidence points to the presence of non&#x02010;motor disturbances, including cognitive and behavioral deficits [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0002" ref-type="bibr">2</xref>]. Cognitive disorders are among the most debilitating non&#x02010;motor symptoms, affecting multiple domains and significantly impacting quality of life. These deficits primarily involve attentional and executive functions, but also episodic memory, language, visuospatial abilities, and social cognition. Like in Alzheimer's disease, cognitive impairments in PD can be classified as mild cognitive impairment (PD&#x02010;MCI) or dementia (PD&#x02010;D), and this classification is based on well&#x02010;defined criteria [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0003" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0004" ref-type="bibr">4</xref>].</p><p>As the disease progresses, the severity of motor symptoms increases, and the intervals between &#x0201c;off&#x0201d; periods shorten. Additionally, pharmacological treatments can lead to motor and non&#x02010;motor fluctuations, prompting the introduction of advanced treatment options [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0005" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>]. Nowadays, these therapies include continuous subcutaneous apomorphine infusion (APO), levodopa&#x02013;carbidopa intestinal gel (LCIG), and deep brain stimulation (DBS). The latter is the most recommended and has garnered the most scientific interest, both for the establishment of selection criteria (CAPSIT&#x02010;PD) [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0006" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>] and for motor and cognitive outcomes [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0007" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0008" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>]. However, not all patients with fluctuations meet the criteria for DBS, or they choose less invasive treatments, the alternatives being APO or LCIG. For these two therapies, no selection criteria are currently available, and the cognitive outcomes following treatment remain unclear. While there are no reviews on the cognitive outcomes of LCIG therapy, a recent one discussed the impact of APO on cognitive functioning in PD [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0009" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>]. Analyzing 23 articles, the authors suggest a generally positive effect of APO, particularly on executive functions, despite evidence of increased cognitive slowing and overall cognitive decline. No adverse behavioral effects are reported. However, the main limitation is the lack of information on the patients' cognitive status before therapy (e.g., cognitive decline before APO treatment), and the inclusion of studies with different follow&#x02010;up intervals (from 20&#x02009;days to 30&#x02009;months) and only a cognitive screening test.</p><p>In this scenario, the aim of this paper was to highlight the long&#x02010;term cognitive outcomes (with at least 6&#x02009;months of follow&#x02010;up) after APO and LCIG in patients with PD, by systematically analyzing the cognitive domains, and the criteria used to characterize the patients' cognitive profile both before and after therapy.</p></sec><sec sec-type="methods" id="ene70077-sec-0006"><label>2</label><title>Methods</title><p>The present review adheres to the guidelines established by the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta&#x02010;Analyses (PRISMA) [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0010" ref-type="bibr">10</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0011" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>]. These guidelines provide an evidence&#x02010;based minimum set of criteria intended to assist authors in accurately reporting systematic reviews.</p><sec id="ene70077-sec-0007"><label>2.1</label><title>Data Sources and Search Strategy</title><p>Four electronic databases (Web of Science, Scopus, MEDLINE via PubMed, and Embase) were searched from inception to January 8, 2025. Only studies published in English were considered. The search terms included the following: &#x0201c;Parkinson,&#x0201d; &#x0201c;Levodopa&#x02010;carbidopa intestinal gel,&#x0201d; &#x0201c;Apomorphine,&#x0201d; and &#x0201c;cognition&#x0201d; (detailed search queries tailored to each specific electronic database are provided in Data&#x000a0;<xref rid="ene70077-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S1</xref>). Additionally, we reviewed the reference lists of the included studies to identify further relevant sources.</p></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0008"><label>2.2</label><title>Eligibility Criteria and Studies Selection Process</title><p>Titles, abstracts, and full&#x02010;text articles were screened and evaluated for eligibility by the two authors based on the following criteria: (i) peer&#x02010;reviewed articles, (ii) published in English, (iii) involving PD patients, (iv) focusing on cognitive outcomes following treatment with LCIG or APO, (v) including at least two cognitive tests, (vi) possibility of extracting cognitive test scores; (vii) including long&#x02010;term cognitive follow&#x02010;up (minimum of 6&#x02009;months), and (viii) reporting original data. Articles reporting animal experiments, surveys, single cases, or conference presentations were excluded. Citations were imported into the web&#x02010;based systematic review software Rayyan (<ext-link xlink:href="https://rayyan.qcri.org" ext-link-type="uri">https://rayyan.qcri.org</ext-link>) to identify and remove duplicates. Any uncertainties regarding article inclusion were resolved through discussion between the authors. Studies meeting the eligibility criteria underwent full&#x02010;text screening. Articles without available full texts were excluded from the review.</p></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0009"><label>2.3</label><title>Data Collection Process</title><p>Publications meeting all eligibility criteria were reviewed by one author and, in case of doubts, discussed with the co&#x02010;author. Relevant data pertaining to the review questions were extracted using a Microsoft Excel data charting form (Microsoft Corporation, Redmond, WA). Specifically, these included the following: (i) study characteristics such as study design, and publication year; (ii) demographic and clinical details including sample size, age at onset, sex, educational background, disease duration, disease severity, and Levodopa Equivalent Daily Dose (LEDD); (iii) information about the cognitive tests employed and the formal cognitive diagnosis; (iv) information about the behavioral assessment, and (v) main motor, behavioral and cognitive findings. The data extraction process involved systematically integrating this information.</p></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0010"><label>2.4</label><title>Study Risk of Bias Assessment</title><p>Studies were evaluated using the Newcastle&#x02013;Ottawa Scale (NOS) for cohort studies, an efficient and convenient tool for quality assessment of non&#x02010;randomized studies included in systematic reviews [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0012" ref-type="bibr">12</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0013" ref-type="bibr">13</xref>]. One author (CL) assessed each study design based on eight items divided into three main domains: selection of cases and controls (four items), comparability of the two groups (one item), and outcome (three items). High&#x02010;quality choices were identified with a &#x0201c;star.&#x0201d; A maximum of one star was assigned for each item within the Selection and Exposure/Outcome domains, while a maximum of two stars could be assigned for Comparability. Overall, the scale ranged from zero to nine stars, with nine representing the highest methodological quality.</p></sec></sec><sec sec-type="results" id="ene70077-sec-0011"><label>3</label><title>Results</title><p>According to the search strategy, a total of 1076 articles were identified (237 in Web of Science, 633 in Scopus, 137 in PubMed, and 69 in Embase databases), 288 of which were excluded due to duplication. After screening titles and abstracts, 62 potentially relevant studies were selected for further considerations, while 726 articles clearly did not meet the eligibility criteria and were excluded for the following reasons: 245 focused on animal experiments or artificial models, 181 were reviews, 4 were surveys, 5 were case reports, 291 did not focus on cognitive aspects, and 18 were excluded for other reasons. After a careful full&#x02010;text screening of the 62 selected articles, 49 additional papers were rejected. Specifically, 4 did not include at least two cognitive tests, 1 was not in English, 24 did not focus on cognitive aspects, 16 were conference abstracts, 2 reported other treatments (morphine and slow&#x02010;release levodopa), and 2 did not include a 6&#x02010;month follow&#x02010;up. The remaining 13 studies were considered (5 for APO and 8 for LCIG). Two additional studies on APO were found through a hand search. The detailed paper selection process is summarized in Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="ene70077-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1</xref>.</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="ene70077-fig-0001"><label>FIGURE 1</label><caption><p>Flow diagram of the articles screening and selection process.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ENE-32-e70077-g001" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-1"/></fig><sec id="ene70077-sec-0012"><label>3.1</label><title>Studies Characteristics and Quality Assessment</title><p>Only three out of seven studies for APO and three out of eight for LCIG were case&#x02013;control studies. In both APO and LCIG studies, the control groups consisted of patients undergoing STN&#x02010;DBS [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>]. Only one study included a control group without neurological or psychiatric disorders [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>], and only one included PD patients on different pharmacological treatment [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>].</p><p>NOS scores for quality assessment ranged from 4 to 9, indicating a moderate risk of bias for all the included studies. Specifically, two to four stars were assigned to studies regarding the selection process of cases and controls. Two or three stars were assigned if cognitive decline was present at baseline [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>], or cognitive screening was not administered but this was not clear from the abstract [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>], or if a control group was not included [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>].</p><p>In the comparability section, zero to two stars were assigned, with all studies earning two stars except those without a control group [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>]. In this section, participants' age, disease duration, and severity were considered the most important variables given their potential influence on cognitive outcomes [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0029" ref-type="bibr">29</xref>]. Out of the five studies with a control group, four provided data about the comparability between the two groups for all factors. Only in Alegret et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>] cases and controls were matched only for age and disease duration, while no information was provided about disease severity.</p><p>Regarding the exposure/outcome sections, all studies earned three stars, except for Borgemeester et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>] and Meira et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>], where drop&#x02010;out at follow&#x02010;up exceeded 44%. All follow&#x02010;ups lasted more than 6&#x02009;months, with the longest chosen to assess long&#x02010;term outcomes. The weighted mean follow&#x02010;up was 12.28&#x02009;months for APO and 28.72&#x02009;months for LCIG.</p><p>Specific ratings for each study are provided in Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="ene70077-tbl-0001" ref-type="table">1</xref>.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="ene70077-tbl-0001" content-type="TABLE"><label>TABLE 1</label><caption><p>NOS Scale for cohort studies.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><thead valign="bottom"><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Authors</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Selection (0&#x02013;4)</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Comparability (0&#x02013;2)</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Exposure/Outcome (0&#x02013;3)</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Total (0&#x02013;9)</th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="left" colspan="5" valign="top" rowspan="1">APO</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Alegret et&#x000a0;al. (2004) [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">7</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">De Gaspari et&#x000a0;al. (2006) [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">9</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Drapier et&#x000a0;al. (2012) [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Auffret et&#x000a0;al. (2017) [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">9</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Borgemeester et&#x000a0;al. (2017) [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Houvenaghel et&#x000a0;al. (2018) [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Meira et&#x000a0;al. (2021) [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="5" valign="top" rowspan="1">LCIG</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Merola et&#x000a0;al. (2011) [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Fasano et&#x000a0;al. (2012) [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Zibetti et&#x000a0;al. (2013) [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Sensi et&#x000a0;al. (2014) [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">C&#x000e1;ceres&#x02010;Redondo et&#x000a0;al. (2014) [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Merola et&#x000a0;al. (2016) [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Vallderiola et&#x000a0;al. (2017) [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">9</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Balestrino et&#x000a0;al. (2024) [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02605;&#x02605;&#x02605;</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot id="ene70077-ntgp-0002"><fn id="ene70077-note-0002"><p>
<italic toggle="yes">Note:</italic> The scoring system is based on three key domains: &#x0201c;Selection of cases and controls,&#x0201d; which can receive up to four stars (&#x02605;); &#x0201c;Comparability of the two groups,&#x0201d; with a maximum of two stars; and &#x0201c;Selection and Exposure/Outcome,&#x0201d; which can also receive up to four stars. The total score for each study is nine stars.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0013"><label>3.2</label><title>Participants' Characteristics</title><sec id="ene70077-sec-0014"><label>3.2.1</label><title>APO</title><p>The seven included studies involved 193 patients and 27 controls (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="ene70077-tbl-0002" ref-type="table">2</xref>). Patients weighted mean age was 60.8&#x02009;years while weighted mean education was 5&#x02009;years, with data being available only in one study [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>]. The entire sample included 94 women and 86 men, but in one study gender was not specified [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>]. All patients were diagnosed with PD, but only five studies detailed the diagnostic criteria [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>]. Regarding disease duration, the weighted mean was of 12.35&#x02009;years. To quantify motor disease severity before and after APO treatment, four primary tools were used: the UPDRS&#x02010;III scale [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>], the UPDRS&#x02010;IV scale [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>], the H&#x00026;Y stage [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>], and/or the modified Abnormal Involuntary Movement Score [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>]. Scores on these scales were available in all studies except in Alegret et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>] The weighted mean score in ON phase was 19.64 for the UPDRS&#x02010;III, 7.72 for the UPDRS&#x02010;IV, and the weighted mean H&#x00026;Y was 2.46. No data were available about the modified Abnormal Involuntary Movement Score [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>]. The LEDD was assessed before and after treatment in all studies. At baseline, the weighted mean LEDD was 1197.1&#x02009;mg.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="ene70077-tbl-0002" content-type="TABLE"><label>TABLE 2</label><caption><p>Clinical&#x02010;demographic and motor characteristics of APO studies.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><thead valign="bottom"><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">References</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Study design</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">N</italic> patients</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Age at onset (years)</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Education (years)</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Disease duration at baseline (months)</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Disease severity at baseline</th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Alegret et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Within&#x02010;subjects analysis</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">7 (4F)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">61.14 (7.58)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.00 (3.00)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">13.86 (2.27)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">UPDRS&#x02010;III, AIMS (no scores were available)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">De Gaspari et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Within&#x02010;subjects analysis</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">13</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">59 (13)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10 (5)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">UPDRS&#x02010;III 32.1&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;7.3</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Drapier et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Within&#x02010;subjects analysis</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">23 (7F)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">48.4 (10.5)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">13.9 (8.2)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">UPDRS&#x02010;III&#x02009;=&#x02009;18.3 (8.3), UPDRS&#x02010;IV&#x02009;=&#x02009;7.7 (3.8), H&#x00026;Y&#x02009;=&#x02009;2.3 (0.9)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Auffret et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Within&#x02010; and between&#x02010;subjects analysis</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">12 (5F)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">65.9 (7.2)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">13.8 (3.6)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">UPDRS&#x02010;III&#x02009;=&#x02009;17.7 (6.2), UPDRS&#x02010;IV&#x02009;=&#x02009;8.1 (2.8), H&#x00026;Y&#x02009;=&#x02009;2.3 (0.6)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Borgemeester et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Within&#x02010;subjects analysis</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">45 (19F)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">70.9 (8.1)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10.8 (4.8)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">H&#x00026;Y&#x02009;=&#x02009;3.2 (1)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Houvenaghel et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Within&#x02010;subjects analysis</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">22 (14&#x02009;F)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">57.5 (9.6)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">11.1 (4.4)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">UPDRS&#x02010;III&#x02009;=&#x02009;11.1 (8.1), UPDRS&#x02010;IV&#x02009;=&#x02009;7.0 (3.3), H&#x00026;Y&#x02009;=&#x02009;1.4 (0.8)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Meira et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Within&#x02010;subjects analysis</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">71 (45F)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">63 (9.7)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">13 (5.9)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">UPDRS&#x02010;III&#x02009;=&#x02009;19 (13), UPDRS&#x02010;IV&#x02009;=&#x02009;8.1 (3.5), H&#x00026;Y&#x02009;=&#x02009;3.1 (1.1)</td></tr></tbody></table><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><thead valign="bottom"><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">References</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">LEDD (mg) at baseline</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Inclusion/Exclusion criteria for therapy</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Control group</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Cognitive follow&#x02010;up (months)</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Adverse events and/or drop&#x02010;outs</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Main motor outcomes</th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Alegret et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1462.86 (448.88)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Exclusion criteria: dementia, severe systemic or psychiatric illness, &#x0003e;&#x02009;75&#x02009;years old, severe brain atrophy or vascular lesions, and insufficient levodopa challenge test</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Nine STN&#x02010;DBS (but none between&#x02010;subjects analysis were performed)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 and 12</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Two drop&#x02010;outs:
<list list-type="bullet" id="ene70077-list-0001"><list-item id="ene70077-li-0001"><p>1 visual hallucination</p></list-item><list-item id="ene70077-li-0002"><p>1 poor acceptance of treatment</p></list-item></list>
</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Improvement in both groups, but dyskinesia severity was reduced significantly only in STN&#x02010;DBS patients</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">De Gaspari et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">665.98 (215)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CAPSIT&#x02010;PD</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">12 STN&#x02010;DBS (but none between&#x02010;subjects analysis were performed)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">12</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">None</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Reduction in daily off time, no changes in AIMS and UPDRS&#x02010;III scores. Reduction of LEDD</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Drapier et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1372.2 (325.1)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Inclusion criteria: PD diagnosis (UK Brain Bank), MDRS&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;131, and not eligible for DBS</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">None</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">12</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">All presented small nodules, some nausea or orthostatic hypotension. No dropouts</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Motor improvement (UPDRS&#x02010;III), LEDD reduction</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Auffret et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1226.8 (422.6)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Inclusion criteria: PD diagnosis (UK Brain Bank) and not eligible for DBS surgery</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 PD on BMT</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Eight patients presented adverse events:
<list list-type="bullet" id="ene70077-list-0002"><list-item id="ene70077-li-0003"><p>Three visual and/or auditory hallucinations</p></list-item></list>
</p>
<p>No dropouts</p>
</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Motor improvement (H&#x00026;Y and UPDRS&#x02010;IV), LEDD reduction</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Borgemeester et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1269 (470)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Inclusion criteria: PD diagnosis and not eligible for DBS</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">26</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>No cognitive or behavioral side effects</p>
<p>20 drop&#x02010;outs</p>
</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Improvement of ON&#x02010;time duration, in absence of dyskinesia duration changes</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Houvenaghel et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1087.7 (485.5)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Exclusion criteria: dementia or MCI</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>One patient developed ICD, one patient developed mild simple visual hallucinations</p>
<p>No dropouts</p>
</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Reduction of LEDD; Reduction of motor fluctuations (score in UPDRS&#x02010;IV)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Meira et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1299 (461)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">Inclusion criteria</italic>: PD diagnosis, routinely medical evaluation for APO treatment</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3, 6, 12, 24</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Adverse effects such as confusion, hallucinations, ICD, and insomnia</p>
<p>39 patients discontinued within the 2&#x02009;years of follow&#x02010;up:
<list list-type="bullet" id="ene70077-list-0003"><list-item id="ene70077-li-0004"><p>Severe psychosis or hallucinations;</p></list-item><list-item id="ene70077-li-0005"><p>Severe ICDs;</p></list-item><list-item id="ene70077-li-0006"><p>Excessive somnolence;</p></list-item><list-item id="ene70077-li-0007"><p>Cognitive deterioration</p></list-item><list-item id="ene70077-li-0008"><p>Other no behavioral reasons</p></list-item></list>
</p>
</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Reduction of motor fluctuations (score in UPDRS&#x02010;IV)</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot id="ene70077-ntgp-0003"><fn id="ene70077-note-0003"><p>Abbreviations: ADL, activity of daily life; AIMS, Abnormal Involuntary Movement Scale; BMT, best medical treatment; FOG, freezing of gait; H&#x00026;Y, Hohen &#x00026; Yahr; LEDD, levodopa equivalent daily dose; OMT, oral medical therapy; STN&#x02010;DBS, subthalamic&#x02010;deep brain stimulation; UPDRS&#x02010;III, Unified Parkinson's Disease Rating Scale.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0015"><label>3.2.2</label><title>LCIG</title><p>The eight included studies involved 179 patients and 20 control subjects (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="ene70077-tbl-0003" ref-type="table">3</xref>). The weighted mean age was 65.1&#x02009;years, while education was never reported. There were 39 women, but in two studies, gender was not specified [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>]. All patients were diagnosed with PD, but only four studies provided detailed diagnostic criteria [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>]. The weighted mean disease duration was 13.66&#x02009;years.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="ene70077-tbl-0003" content-type="TABLE"><label>TABLE 3</label><caption><p>Clinical&#x02010;demographic and motor characteristics of LCIG studies.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><thead valign="bottom"><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">References</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Study design</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">N</italic> patients</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Age at onset (years)</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Education (years)</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Disease duration at baseline (months)</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Disease severity at baseline</th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Merola et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Within&#x02010; and between&#x02010;subjects analysis</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">20 (7F)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">69 (5.9)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">13.9 (4.5)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">UPDRS&#x02010;III&#x02009;=&#x02009;24.2 (10.56), UPDRS&#x02010;IV&#x02009;=&#x02009;8.55 (4.2)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Fasano et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Within&#x02010;subject analysis</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">14 (4F)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">67.1 (11.5)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">12.9 (4.8)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">UPDRS&#x02010;III and IV (specific scores not available, but inferable from figure&#x000a0;2)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Zibetti et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Within&#x02010;subject analysis</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">25 (9F)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">69.9 (5.8)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">12.1 (4.1)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">UPDRS&#x02010;III&#x02009;=&#x02009;23.2 (9.2), UPDRS&#x02010;IV&#x02009;=&#x02009;8.4 (3.2)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Sensi et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Within&#x02010;subject analysis</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">17 (7F)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">67.6 (6.1)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15.47 (4.04)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">H&#x00026;Y&#x02009;=&#x02009;3.17 (0.8), UPDRS&#x02010;III&#x02009;=&#x02009;35.5 (11.5), UPDRS&#x02010;IV&#x02009;=&#x02009;8.4 (2.5)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">C&#x000e1;ceres&#x02010;Redondo et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Within&#x02010;subject analysis</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">16 (9F) (only 9 had a full cognitive follow&#x02010;up)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">64.5 (9.0)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">14.1 (3.9)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">UPDRS&#x02010;III&#x02009;=&#x02009;27.2 (8.5), UPDRS&#x02010;IV&#x02009;=&#x02009;8.7 (2.3)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Merola et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Within&#x02010; and between&#x02010;subject analysis</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">20</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">64.60 (6.99)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">13.75 (2.57)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">UPDRS&#x02010;III&#x02009;=&#x02009;19.94 (11.36), UPDRS&#x02010;IV&#x02009;=&#x02009;7.89 (2.37)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Valldeoriola et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Within&#x02010;subject analysis</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">11 (3F)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">64</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">14.5</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">H&#x00026;Y&#x02009;=&#x02009;2.5; UPDRS&#x02010;III&#x02009;=&#x02009;22, UPDRS&#x02010;IV&#x02009;=&#x02009;6 (3)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Balestrino et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Within&#x02010;subject analysis</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">56 (but 49 with cognitive assessment, 32 with neuropsychological follow&#x02010;up)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">54.3 (7.9)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">12.6 (4.1)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">H&#x00026;Y&#x02009;=&#x02009;2.6; UPDRS&#x02010;III&#x02009;=&#x02009;12.6 (5.4), UPDRS&#x02010;IV&#x02009;=&#x02009;9.4 (2.9)</td></tr></tbody></table><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><thead valign="bottom"><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">References</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">LEDD (mg) at baseline</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Inclusion/Exclusion criteria</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Control group</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Cognitive follow&#x02010;up (months)</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Adverse events and/or drop&#x02010;outs</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Main motor outcomes</th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Merola et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">994.5 (268)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">Inclusion criteria</italic>: absence of criteria for DBS surgery</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">20 STN&#x02010;DBS</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">14.7 (7.6)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Thirteen patients experienced one or more complications, related to the infusion system. Only one psychotic episode.</p>
<p>No drop&#x02010;outs</p>
</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Improvement in motor symptoms and complications (UPDRS&#x02010;IV)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Fasano et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">Inclusion criteria</italic>: Sever motor fluctuations refractory, and good levodopa responsiveness</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">None</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">24.9 (14.4)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Two ICDs, but less severe than before LCIG</p>
<p>No dropouts</p>
</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Reduction of motor fluctuations and dyskinesia (UPDRS&#x02010;IV)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Zibetti et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1021 (269)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">Inclusion criteria</italic>: Sever motor fluctuations refractory, and good levodopa responsiveness; <italic toggle="yes">Exclusion criteria</italic>: dementia</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">None</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">36.2 (11.5)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>All patients experienced adverse events, such as severe psychosis</p>
<p>No dropouts were specificated</p>
</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Reduction of LEDD; Improvement of motor symptoms (UPDRS&#x02010;IV)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Sensi et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1158.9 (334.5)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">Inclusion criteria</italic>: Summary of Product Characteristics criteria. <italic toggle="yes">Exclusion criteria</italic>: Severe PD&#x02010;related psychosis and/or severe hallucinations, dementia</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">None</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 and 24</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>14 patients manifested at least one adverse event, such as hallucinations, agitations, and mood disturbances.</p>
<p>3 dropouts: 1 severe psychosis.</p>
<p>One patient died for LCIG probably related problems</p>
</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Improvement of FOG; Reduction motor fluctuations and dyskinesia (UPDRS&#x02010;IV)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">C&#x000e1;ceres&#x02010;Redondo et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1306.1 (337.6)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">Inclusion criteria</italic>: motor fluctuations and dyskinesia. <italic toggle="yes">Exclusion criteria</italic>: Severe dementia, acute psychotic disorder or other substantial medical problems</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">None</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">32.2 (12.4)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>29 patients experienced almost one adverse event, such as psychosis</p>
<p>13 patients not completed the follow&#x02010;up</p>
<p>7 dropouts: 1 hallucination with persistent psychosis</p>
<p>Two patients died for causes considered unrelated to LCIG</p>
</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Reduction of motor fluctuations and dyskinesia (UPDRS&#x02010;IV)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Merola et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1272 (432)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">Inclusion criteria</italic>: CAPSIT&#x02010;PD, not treated with STN&#x02010;DBS</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">20 STN&#x02010;DBS, 20 OMT</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">61.8 (17.52)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>20 patients manifested almost one adverse event, but not related to behavioral disturbances</p>
<p>No drop&#x02010;outs were specificated</p>
</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Reduction of OFF times, severity and duration of dyskinesia (UPDRS&#x02010;IV)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Valldeoriola et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1145 (305)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>
<italic toggle="yes">Inclusion criteria</italic>: possible candidates for advanced therapies such as DBS or LCIG; MMSE &#x02265;&#x02009;24; SCOPS&#x02010;Cog &#x02265;&#x02009;19</p>
<p>
<italic toggle="yes">Exclusion criteria</italic>: dementia, major psychiatric illnesses, and/or progressive medical illnesses</p>
</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">12 STN&#x02010;DBS, 23 HC</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 and 12</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Only one patient manifested adverse event related to the pump</p>
<p>No dropouts</p>
</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Reduction of motor complications (UPDRS&#x02010;IV)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Balestrino et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1164.56 (438.1)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">Inclusion criteria</italic>: Fulfillment of inclusion criteria for LCIG therapy</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">None</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">24 (12)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10 drop&#x02010;outs for complications, such as psychosis in GBA1 patient</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Worsening in H&#x00026;Y, UPDRS&#x02010;III and LEDD; Reduction of motor complications (UPDRS&#x02010;IV)</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot id="ene70077-ntgp-0004"><fn id="ene70077-note-0004"><p>Abbreviations: FOG, freezing of gait; H&#x00026;Y, Hohen &#x00026; Yahr; EDD, levodopa equivalent daily dose; OMT, oral medical therapy; STN&#x02010;DBS, subthalamic&#x02010;deep brain stimulation; UPDRS&#x02010;III, Unified Parkinson's Disease Rating Scale.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><p>To quantify motor disease severity before and after LCIG treatment, three primary tools were used: the UPDRS&#x02010;III scale [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>], the UPDRS&#x02010;IV scale [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>], and/or the H&#x00026;Y stage [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>]. Scores on these scales were available in all studies except for Fasano et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>], where, however, a graph allows to infer values.</p><p>The weighted mean score in the ON phase was 21.52 for the UPDRS&#x02010;III, 8.19 for the UPDRS&#x02010;IV, while the weighted mean H&#x00026;Y was 2.75.</p><p>LEDD was assessed before and after starting treatment in all studies, except in Fasano et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>] At T0, the weighted mean LEDD was 1151.72&#x02009;mg.</p></sec></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0016"><label>3.3</label><title>Eligibility Criteria for Advanced Therapies</title><sec id="ene70077-sec-0017"><label>3.3.1</label><title>APO</title><p>Two studies specifically excluded patients with dementia [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>]. One study ruled out patients with severe brain atrophy or vascular lesions [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>], while another excluded patients because of significant psychiatric issues [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>]. Additionally, three studies included patients who were not eligible for DBS [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>]. Only one study applied the CAPSIT&#x02010;PD criteria for patient selection [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>], whereas another did not provide specific details on the selection process [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>].</p></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0018"><label>3.3.2</label><title>LCIG</title><p>Five studies explicitly excluded patients with dementia [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>]. Four studies highlighted that patients had to experience motor fluctuations refractory to conventional medication [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>]. Three studies included only patients with a good response to levodopa [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>]. Additionally, two studies included patients if they were not candidates for DBS, as determined by CAPSIT&#x02010;PD screening [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>]. Furthermore, three studies deemed patients with psychiatric disorders ineligible [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>], while only two studies listed the absence of a caregiver as an exclusion criterion [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>].</p></sec></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0019"><label>3.4</label><title>Adverse Events and/or Dropouts</title><sec id="ene70077-sec-0020"><label>3.4.1</label><title>APO</title><p>Six out of seven studies reported adverse effects and/or dropouts. Specifically, Alegret et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>] noted one patient out of nine drops out due to the onset of hallucinations. In Drapier et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>] all patients experienced side effects related to the pump, but no behavioral disturbances. In Auffret et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>] eight patients showed visual or auditory hallucinations, but these were not severe enough to interrupt the treatment. In Borgemeester et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>], no behavioral side effects were described and 20 patients were lost to follow&#x02010;up for different reasons, one dying from causes unrelated to APO.</p><p>Houvenaghel et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>] reported that, out of 22 patients, one developed ICD and another presented visual hallucinations. In Meira et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>] 39 patients (34%) discontinued treatment within 2&#x02009;years due to different reasons, including hallucinations, severe ICD, or cognitive decline.</p></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0021"><label>3.4.2</label><title>LCIG</title><p>In all studies, several patients experienced adverse events, with reported rates as follows: 65% in Merola et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>], 14% in Fasano et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>], 100% in Zibetti et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>], 82% in Sensi et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>], 100% in Merola et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>], and 9% in Vallderiola et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>].</p><p>Dropouts were mainly due to psychosis, namely three in Sensi et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>] (out of 20), one in C&#x000e1;ceres&#x02010;Redondo et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>] (out of 16), and ten in Balestrino et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>] (out of 56) for different reasons, including psychosis.</p></sec></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0022"><label>3.5</label><title>Behavioral Assessment</title><sec id="ene70077-sec-0023"><label>3.5.1</label><title>APO</title><p>All included studies assessed the presence of behavioral symptoms both before and after starting therapy, apart from Drapier et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>] and Borgemeester et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>] Generic scales were employed (such as the Neuropsychiatric Inventory, NPI) [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>] along with specific scales for depression [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>], apathy [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>], and/or anxiety [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>]. More details are provided in Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="ene70077-tbl-0004" ref-type="table">4</xref>.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="ene70077-tbl-0004" content-type="TABLE"><label>TABLE 4</label><caption><p>Cognitive characteristics of APO and LCIG studies.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><thead valign="bottom"><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" colspan="6" valign="bottom" rowspan="1">APO</th></tr><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">References</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Cognitive screening test score &#x02013; mean (sd)</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Formal cognitive diagnosis</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Other cognitive tests</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Behavioral questionnaire</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Main cognitive and behavioral outcomes</th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Alegret et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MMSE&#x02009;=&#x02009;27.86 (1.57)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">None</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">RAVLT, Stroop, TMT, Fluency, JLO</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">BDI</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">No cognitive and behavioral changes</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">De Gaspari et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MMSE&#x02009;=&#x02009;28.32 (2.49)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">None</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CVLT. Corsi test</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">NPI, HDRS</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Corsi test worsening</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Drapier et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MDRS&#x02009;=&#x02009;129.9 (10.0)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">None</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">TMT, Stroop, Fluency, WCST</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">TMT A worsening</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Auffret et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MDRS&#x02009;=&#x02009;136.6 (5.1)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">None</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">TMT, Stroop, Fluency, WCST</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MADRS, STAI, LARS</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Executive improvement (WCST)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Borgemeester et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MMSE&#x02009;=&#x02009;22.7 (5.0)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Dubois et&#x000a0;al. (2007)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">FAB or SCOPA&#x02010;COG</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">No cognitive changes</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Houvenaghel et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MDRS&#x02009;=&#x02009;36.6 (0.7)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Level I MDS criteria (Litvan et&#x000a0;al., 2012)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Phonemic and semantic fluency, MCST, TMT, Stroop</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">AES, AMDPAT, MADRS</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">No cognitive changes. One patient developed ICD, one patient develop slight simple visual hallucinations</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Meira et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MMSE&#x02009;=&#x02009;27 (3)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">None</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">FAB</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">ASBPD</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MMSE and FAB worsening</td></tr></tbody></table><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><thead valign="bottom"><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" colspan="6" valign="bottom" rowspan="1">LCIG</th></tr><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">References</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Cognitive screening test score &#x02013; mean (sd)</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Formal cognitive diagnosis</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Cognitive tests</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Psychological questionnaire</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Main cognitive and psychological outcomes</th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Merola et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">None</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">None</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">TMT B, Phonemic fluency, Digit Span, Corsi, Paired associative learning, CPM47</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">BDI</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Worsening of Phonemic fluency. No behavioral changes</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Fasano et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MMSE&#x02009;=&#x02009;22.20 (5.55)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">None</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">FAB</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">NPI, QUIP, AES</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">No cognitive changes, but improvement of psychiatric symptoms in QUIP, but not in NPI</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Zibetti et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MMSE ProD&#x02009;=&#x02009;24.7 (2.7); no&#x02010;ProD&#x02009;=&#x02009;27.3 (0.9)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Petersen et&#x000a0;al. (1999) for PD&#x02010;MCI, Emre et&#x000a0;al. (2007) for PD&#x02010;D</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CPM47, BWR, CBT, RAVLT, Digit Cancelation Test, TMT&#x02010;A, TMT&#x02010;B, FAB, Clock, phonemic and semantic fluency</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">BDI, MAS</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Worsening of global cognition (15 PD&#x02010;MCI; 7 PDD). Specifically, worsening of MMSE, CMP47, Corsi, RAVLT, Digit Cancelation test, TMT&#x02010;A, TMT B, FAB, Clock, and phonemic and semantic fluency. No behavioral changes</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Sensi et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MMSE&#x02009;=&#x02009;25.0 (2.7)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">DSM&#x02010;IV both for PD&#x02010;MCI and PD&#x02010;D</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">FAB</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">NPI</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Worsening of MMSE only in PD&#x02010;D. No behavioral changes</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">C&#x000e1;ceres&#x02010;Redondo et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MMSE&#x02009;=&#x02009;24.7 (3.5)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Petersen et&#x000a0;al. (1999) for PD&#x02010;MCI, Emre et&#x000a0;al. (2007) for PD&#x02010;D</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">DRS</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">NPI&#x02010;Q</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Worsening of MMSE and DRS. 1 PD&#x02010;MCI, 4 PD&#x02010;D. No behavioral changes</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Merola et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MMSE&#x02009;=&#x02009;29.33 (0.65)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Level I MDS criteria (Litvan et&#x000a0;al. 2012) for PD&#x02010;MCI, Emre et&#x000a0;al. (2007) for PD&#x02010;D</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MoCA. Only patients from one Centre underwent a more deeper battery: verbal span, spatial span, paired associative learning, phonemic and semantic fluency, TMT B, Nelson's associative categories</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">/</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Worsening of cognition (30% PD&#x02010;MCI vs. 5% at baseline; 25% PD&#x02010;D vs. 0% at baseline)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Valldeoriola et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MMSE&#x02009;=&#x02009;28</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">None</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">SCOPA&#x02010;Cog, WMS&#x02010;III, semantic and phonemic fluency, Stroop test, JLO, Orientation test</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">BDI&#x02010;II, LARS, BIS&#x02010;30, MOCI</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Worsening of SCOPA&#x02010;Cog and Stroop test. Improvement of WMS learning, recall, recognition, Benton Judgment of Line Orientation test. No behavioral changes</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Balestrino et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MMSE&#x02009;=&#x02009;27.6 (1.8)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Level I MDS criteria (Litvan et&#x000a0;al. 2012) for PD&#x02010;MCI, Emre et&#x000a0;al. (2007) for PD&#x02010;D</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CPM47, RAVLT, TMT, MCST, phonemic and semantic fluency, DCT, FAB, CBT, DS, CDT</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">BDI, MAS</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">No cognitive or behavioral changes</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot id="ene70077-ntgp-0005"><fn id="ene70077-note-0005"><p>Abbreviations: AES, Apathy Evaluation Scale; AMDPAT, AMDP&#x02010;AT Anxiety Scale; ASBPD, Ardouin Scale of Behavior in Parkinson's Disease; BDI, Beck Depression Scale; BIS&#x02010;30, Barratt Impulsivity Scale&#x02010;30; BWR, Words Rep. test; CBT, Corsi's Tapping Test; CDT, Clock Drawing Test; CPM47, Color Progressive Matrices; CVLT, California Verbal Learning Test; DCT, Digit Cancelation Test; DRS, Dementia Rating Scale; DS, Digit Span; FAB, Frontal Assessment Battery; HDRS, Hamilton Depression Rating Scale; JLO, Judgment Line Orientation; LARS, Lille Apathy Rating Scale; MADRS, Montgomery&#x02010;Asberg Depression Rating Scale; MAS, Marin Apathy Scale; MCST, Modified Card Sorting Test; MMSE, Mini Mental State Examination; MOCI, Maudsley Obsessional&#x02010;Compulsive Questionnaire; NPI, Neuropsychiatric Inventory; QUIP, Questionnaire for Impulsive&#x02010;Compulsive Disorders in Parkinson's Disease; RAVLT, Rey Auditory&#x02010;Verbal Learning Test; SCOPA&#x02010;COG, Scale for Outcomes in Parkinson's Disease&#x02010;COGnition; STAI, State&#x02013;Trait Anxiety Inventory; TMT, Trail Making Test; WCST, Wisconsin Card Sorting Test; WMS&#x02010;III, Wechsler Memory Scale&#x02010;III.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0024"><label>3.5.2</label><title>LCIG</title><p>All included studies assessed the presence of behavioral symptoms both before and after starting therapy, apart from Merola et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>]. Generic scales were employed (NPI) [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>] along with specific scales for the evaluation of depression [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>], ICD [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>], anxiety [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>], apathy [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>], and impulsivity [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>]. More details are provided in Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="ene70077-tbl-0004" ref-type="table">4</xref>.</p></sec></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0025"><label>3.6</label><title>Cognitive Tests, Domains, and Criteria</title><sec id="ene70077-sec-0026"><label>3.6.1</label><title>APO</title><p>Four out of five studies employed the Mini&#x02010;Mental State Examination (MMSE) as a screening test [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>], with a weighted mean score of 26.47 (range 0&#x02013;30). Only Drapier et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>], Auffret et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>], and Houvenaghel et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>] used the Mattis Dementia Rating Scale, reporting a mean score of 135.76 (range 0&#x02013;144).</p><p>Regarding the formal diagnosis of cognitive decline, Borgemeester et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>] followed Dubois et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0030" ref-type="bibr">30</xref>]'s criteria, while Houvenaghel et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>] applied the Level I MDS criteria [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0003" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>] to exclude patients with PD&#x02010;MCI or PD&#x02010;D. Specifically, in Borgemeester et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>] the number of patients meeting the criteria for cognitive impairment is not explicitly stated, but all patients were unable to live independently.</p><p>No study conducted a comprehensive Level II assessment. Borgemeester et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>] and Meira et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>] used screening tests (MMSE, SCOPA&#x02010;COG, and FAB), while other studies assessed memory [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>], attentional executive functions [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>], and visuospatial skills [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>]. Cognitive tests to evaluate these different domains varied. Memory was assessed with word learning [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>], and Corsi Span [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>]; attentional executive functions by means of the Stroop Test [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>], Trail Making Test [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>], phonemic verbal fluency [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>], FAB [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>], and Modified Card Sorting Test [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>]. Finally, for visuospatial skills, the Benton Judgment of Line Orientation (JLO) test was employed [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>]. No evaluation of language is reported, as shown in Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="ene70077-tbl-0004" ref-type="table">4</xref>.</p></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0027"><label>3.6.2</label><title>LCIG</title><p>Even in this case, the MMSE was the most used cognitive screening test [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>] reporting a weighted mean score of 26.17 (range 0&#x02013;30). Vallderiola et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>] included an additional screening test, the SCOPA&#x02010;COG, with a mean score of 25 (range 0&#x02013;43). Merola et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>] also used the MoCA, without providing patients' scores. In a previous study [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>] no cognitive screening tests were administered.</p><p>Regarding formal cognitive diagnosis, five out of eight studies employed specific diagnostic criteria or similar approaches. Namely, Zibetti et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>], and C&#x000e1;ceres&#x02010;Redondo et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>] based their classifications on Petersen et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0031" ref-type="bibr">31</xref>], Sensi et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>] followed the DSM&#x02010;IV criteria, while Merola et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>] and Balestrino et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>] referred to the MDS criteria [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0003" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>] for the classification in PD&#x02010;MCI. For the diagnosis of PD&#x02010;D, all studies relied on the criteria established by Emre et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0031" ref-type="bibr">31</xref>] except for Sensi et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>] who used the DSM&#x02010;IV criteria. Applying these different diagnostic criteria, 42 patients (23.5%) were classified PD&#x02010;MCI [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>], and three with dementia (1%) [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>] before therapy.</p><p>Focusing on the specific evaluation of cognitive domains, no study conducted a comprehensive Level II assessment. Fasano et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>], Sensi et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>], and C&#x000e1;ceres&#x02010;Redondo et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>] only used two or more screening tests (MMSE, FAB, and/or DRS). Other authors conducted more in&#x02010;depth assessments of the main cognitive domains such as memory [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>], attention&#x02010;executive functions [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>], visuospatial reasoning [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>], language [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>], and visuospatial abilities [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>]. Crucially, social cognition, which is one of the earliest impaired domains [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0032" ref-type="bibr">32</xref>], was never assessed.</p><p>Studies investigating cognitive outcomes post&#x02010;LCIG used extremely heterogeneous cognitive tests, such as Digit Span [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>], Corsi Span [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>], word learning [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>], and visual recall [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>] for memory; tests for selective attention [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>], divided attention [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>], inhibitory control [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>], verbal flexibility [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>], and categorization [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>] for attentional&#x02010;executive functions; Raven's Progressive Matrices for reasoning [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>]. Language evaluation involved Semantic Verbal Fluency [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>], while visuospatial abilities were assessed using the JLO test [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>]. More details are provided in Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="ene70077-tbl-0004" ref-type="table">4</xref>.</p></sec></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0028"><label>3.7</label><title>Main Motor Outcomes After Advances Therapies</title><sec id="ene70077-sec-0029"><label>3.7.1</label><title>APO</title><p>All studies highlight a motor improvement following treatment. Specifically, they report decrease of motor symptoms [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>], reduction in OFF times, dyskinesias, and/or motor fluctuations [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>]. Additionally, a significant decrease in the LEDD is reported [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>].</p></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0030"><label>3.7.2</label><title>LCIG</title><p>Even following LCIG, motor symptoms improved. General motor improvement [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>] and reduction in motor fluctuations are described [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>]. Conversely, LEDD remained stable&#x000a0;[<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>], except in Balestrino et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>] who report a significant increase in LEDD at follow&#x02010;up.</p></sec></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0031"><label>3.8</label><title>Main Cognitive and Behavioral Outcomes After Advanced Therapies</title><sec id="ene70077-sec-0032"><label>3.8.1</label><title>APO</title><p>Most studies report stability in cognitive function following the introduction of APO [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>], but in four the Corsi span [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>], TMT part A [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>], MMSE, and FAB [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>] declined. Only Auffret et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>] report an improvement in the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test.</p><p>Regarding behavioral changes, only one study [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>] reported ICD and mild simple visual hallucinations in a single patient.</p></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0033"><label>3.8.2</label><title>LCIG</title><p>Unlike APO, cognitive and behavioral outcomes following LCIG show considerable heterogeneity. Half of the studies report a global cognitive decline [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>], which is reflected in a significant worsening in the screening test at follow&#x02010;up compared to baseline. This overall decline corresponds to an increase in PD&#x02010;MCI and PD&#x02010;D. Specifically, Zibetti et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>] identified 15 cases of PD&#x02010;MCI and 7 cases of PD&#x02010;D (compared to 7 patients diagnosed with probable dementia at T0). C&#x000e1;ceres&#x02010;Redondo et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>] reported 1 case of PD&#x02010;MCI and 4 cases of PD&#x02010;D (compared to 4 PD&#x02010;MCI and 0 PD&#x02010;D at T0). Merola et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>] found that 30% of patients met the criteria for PD&#x02010;MCI (vs. 5% at T0) and 25% for PD&#x02010;D (vs. 0% at T0). Interestingly, Sensi et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>] noted a decline in global functioning only in patients who exhibited dementia already at T0. Finally, Merola et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>] observed a deterioration only in the verbal fluency test, but no screening test is reported.</p><p>In general, no behavioral changes were reported [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>], except in one study where an improvement was observed, reflected in lower scores at the QUIP, though not on the NPI [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>].</p></sec></sec></sec><sec sec-type="discussion" id="ene70077-sec-0034"><label>4</label><title>Discussion</title><p>This study systematically reviewed cognitive outcomes following two advanced therapies for PD, namely APO and LCIG. We thoroughly address which cognitive selection criteria were used, how cognitive domains were evaluated, and how a diagnosis of cognitive decline was performed before and after therapy. Nevertheless, this review relied on a relatively limited number of studies, with different measures of outcome. For these reasons a meta&#x02010;analysis was not conducted.</p><sec id="ene70077-sec-0035"><label>4.1</label><title>Cognitive Outcomes of <styled-content style="fixed-case" toggle="no">APO</styled-content>
</title><p>All studies demonstrated motor improvements and confirmed reductions in LEDD after APO therapy [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0033" ref-type="bibr">33</xref>].</p><p>Cognitive functioning at an average follow&#x02010;up of 16&#x02009;months seems to be stable, although some studies indicated declines in visuospatial short&#x02010;term memory and executive functions. However, cognitive profiles were not in&#x02010;depth evaluated before and after therapy, relying mostly on screening tests with lower sensitivity compared to comprehensive assessments [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0034" ref-type="bibr">34</xref>]. This could have underestimated cognitive changes. The reported executive decline in one study aligns with established findings that APO affects brain activity in the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0035" ref-type="bibr">35</xref>]. A recent review also noted slowing of information processing following APO therapy [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0009" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>]. However, discrepancies persist. For instance, Auffret et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>] reported executive improvement on the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test, and another study not included in our review also noted similar enhancements in early&#x02010;stage PD patients [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0036" ref-type="bibr">36</xref>].</p><p>Attentional executive functions were most frequently assessed, but cognitive decline can impact various domains, leading to different cognitive profiles&#x000a0;[<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0037" ref-type="bibr">37</xref>]. Notably, no studies systematically evaluated episodic memory, language, or social cognition. Given that APO may induce oxidative damage affecting structures like the hippocampus [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0038" ref-type="bibr">38</xref>], evaluating episodic memory in both verbal and visual modalities is crucial. Additionally, documented deficits in semantic verbal fluency, lexical access, emotion recognition, and theory of mind [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0032" ref-type="bibr">32</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0039" ref-type="bibr">39</xref>] underscore the need for a comprehensive assessment of these domains before and after therapy to accurately identify potential cognitive side effects.</p></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0036"><label>4.2</label><title>Behavioral Outcomes of <styled-content style="fixed-case" toggle="no">APO</styled-content>
</title><p>A recent study [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0040" ref-type="bibr">40</xref>] reports that while ICDs are associated with all dopamine agonists, the frequency is lower for APO and risk of developing ICDs is relatively low: the results from the TOLEDO study showed that these cases were mild in severity, reversible and uncommon (&#x0003c;&#x02009;10%). In particular, when screening scales were used no changes were detected. This evidence is in line with Houvenaghel et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0009" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>] who found that most studies did not document significant behavioral changes following APO treatment. In fact, behavioral symptoms, such as hallucinations and ICD, are observed without an objective assessment and an indication of the behavioral status before therapy. Only few studies included in this review provide this information. For example, in Borgemeester et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>] 84% of patients had a previous history of hallucinations, 71% of active hallucinations, and 20% dementia. The same authors instead suggest a beneficial effect of APO in the treatment of visual hallucinations in PD, that they explain with the capacity to reduce serotoninergic activity. In fact, a randomized controlled trial of the impact of APO on visual hallucinations is lacking. Of course, APO has to be used properly with respect to the cholinergic deficits and therefore it should be prescribed with caution in PD patients with significant cognitive impairment.</p><p>It is the persistence of these symptoms, probably already present, that has led to discontinuation of APO therapy [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0041" ref-type="bibr">41</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0042" ref-type="bibr">42</xref>]. Although the presence of behavioral symptoms does not constitute a contraindication for APO therapy, the evidence suggests that behavioral side effects must be carefully monitored in patients undergoing APO.</p></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0037"><label>4.3</label><title>Cognitive Outcomes of <styled-content style="fixed-case" toggle="no">LCIG</styled-content>
</title><p>As well established in the literature, motor symptoms improved following LCIG. The benefit and safety of LCIG on motor function are well described in the review and meta&#x02010;analysis by Wang and Chen [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0043" ref-type="bibr">43</xref>]. In fact, the authors provide evidence that continuous infusion of LCIG led to a clinically significant increase in &#x0201c;on&#x0201d; time without troublesome dyskinesia in patients with advanced PD, compared to immediate&#x02010;release oral levodopa. Additionally, this benefit was associated with a significant reduction in &#x0201c;off&#x0201d; time.</p><p>Despite improvements in motor symptoms, LEDD generally remained stable, but also a significant increase in LEDD at follow&#x02010;up is reported [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>]. This evidence may seem surprising as LCIG, with its continuous release of levodopa, should improve therapeutic efficacy and reduce fluctuations without requiring high doses. Nonetheless, a review by the European Academy of Neurology (EAN) did not find a significant reduction in medication use following LCIG [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0007" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>].</p><p>While studies in early&#x02010;stage patients have shown that levodopa enhances prefrontal cortex activity, improves cognition, and induces changes in the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0044" ref-type="bibr">44</xref>], these effects are linked to preserved orbital fronto&#x02010;striatal circuits and reduced dopaminergic striatal stimulation [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0045" ref-type="bibr">45</xref>]. In advanced stages, as in LCIG patients, progressive dopamine depletion and long&#x02010;term changes in brain circuits may alter these dynamics [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0046" ref-type="bibr">46</xref>], potentially leading to different cognitive outcomes.</p><p>In fact, despite a general stability of LEDD after LCIG, we found cognitive decline at an average follow&#x02010;up of 28.72&#x02009;months, as assessed by means of the MMSE. Notably, none of the studies performed detailed neuropsychological assessments or used tests specifically targeting the cognitive domains most affected in PD. Attentional and executive functions were the most frequently assessed, followed by memory, language, logical deductive reasoning, and visuo&#x02010;spatial skills. Social cognition was never evaluated. Compared to APO research, LCIG studies seem to have a broader focus on cognitive aspects, with a larger number of tests and better patients' classification. Data suggest that cognitive decline is prevalent among patients already with PD&#x02010;D before therapy, with an increasing percentage of patients showing deficits over time. Nonetheless, variability in cognitive diagnostic criteria across studies may impact results, highlighting the need for combining data from studies with consistent criteria to better understand cognitive impairment in PD after LCIG.</p></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0038"><label>4.4</label><title>Behavioral Outcomes of <styled-content style="fixed-case" toggle="no">LCIG</styled-content>
</title><p>According to the examined studies, behavior tends to remain relatively stable, with no significant differences in pre&#x02010; and post&#x02010;therapy scores on behavioral questionnaires.</p><p>However, several studies qualitatively report the onset of psychosis in patients undergoing LCIG. The mechanism underlying the onset of psychosis following LCIG remains unclear, but it is thought to arise from a combination of factors. One key factor is excessive dopaminergic stimulation, likely due to the continuous infusion of the medication, which contrasts with the fluctuating levels seen in traditional administration. This sustained stimulation may disrupt neural regulation, leading to dysfunction in brain regions responsible for perception and behavior [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0047" ref-type="bibr">47</xref>]. Thakkar et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0048" ref-type="bibr">48</xref>] suggested that hallucinations can increase when flushing the tube before bedtime. Additionally, the disease duration itself may predict psychosis [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0047" ref-type="bibr">47</xref>] and, recently, significantly more hallucinations have been reported in GBA1 PD [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0049" ref-type="bibr">49</xref>]. Together, these factors could increase the likelihood of developing psychotic symptoms, such as hallucinations and delusions, in some patients undergoing LCIG.</p><p>But even in the case of LCIG there are conflicting results: Ricciardi et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0050" ref-type="bibr">50</xref>] reported a decrease in hallucinations compared to the baseline. ICDs are even less described and reported as mild: in a series of 67 patients only 5 without a history of ICDs developed a mild form and discontinuation was due to different adverse effects.</p><p>Based on these reports, it is crucial to evaluate behavioral aspects in patients undergoing advanced therapies. This assessment helps ensure the suitability of the treatment and enables monitoring of psychological well&#x02010;being. Such attention is particularly important due to the potential behavioral side effects associated with levodopa therapy [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0051" ref-type="bibr">51</xref>].</p></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0039"><label>4.5</label><title>Final Considerations and Future Needs</title><p>Overall, from this systematic review on the cognitive effects of APO and LCIG, it emerges that APO appears to be &#x0201c;safer&#x0201d; than LCIG in terms of cognitive effects.</p><p>However, it is important to note that studies on APO generally have shorter follow&#x02010;up periods and are less thorough compared to those on LCIG. This limited approach may overlook potential cognitive changes associated with APO therapy.</p><p>Furthermore, considering the moderate risk of bias of the included studies, none of which is a randomized controlled trial (RCT), caution is warranted when generalizing these results to clinical practice. Further studies with higher methodological rigor and more careful attention to the cognitive selection of patients are necessary to clarify this issue.</p><p>Finally, it is important to highlight the key issues that emerge from this systematic review and that require attention:
<list list-type="bullet" id="ene70077-list-0004"><list-item id="ene70077-li-0009"><p>There are no selection criteria for these therapies, either motor or cognitive. This gap inevitably leads to studies that lack rigor in patient selection, resulting in heterogeneity of the findings.</p></list-item><list-item id="ene70077-li-0010"><p>No study has performed a Level II cognitive assessment. It is essential to offer candidate patients a comprehensive battery of tests that includes at least one screening test and/or two tests for each cognitive domain [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0003" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>]. Several batteries have been developed that require no more than 90&#x02009;min and are sensitive in discriminating the presence or absence of MCI [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0034" ref-type="bibr">34</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0052" ref-type="bibr">52</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0053" ref-type="bibr">53</xref>, <xref rid="ene70077-bib-0054" ref-type="bibr">54</xref>]. Alternatively, Level I batteries are available with good diagnostic capabilities [<xref rid="ene70077-bib-0034" ref-type="bibr">34</xref>].</p></list-item><list-item id="ene70077-li-0011"><p>As a consequence of the previous point, greater rigor in the application of criteria for the diagnosis of PD&#x02010;MCI or PD&#x02010;D is needed. This would allow a better characterization of patients prior to treatment and a clearer understanding of any potential changes in their cognitive status.</p></list-item></list>
</p><p>To conclude, cognitive assessment plays a crucial role in managing PD patients, and it should be considered especially when advanced therapies are involved. A detailed cognitive evaluation can predict treatment outcomes and help tailor interventions to improve the patient's quality of life, reduce caregiver's strain, and prevent therapy discontinuation due to cognitive or behavioral side effects. Therefore, prioritizing comprehensive cognitive assessments is essential for optimizing patient's care and achieving successful treatment results.</p></sec></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0044"><title>Author Contributions</title><p>
<bold>Chiara Longo:</bold> conceptualization, methodology, visualization, writing &#x02013; original draft, investigation. <bold>Costanza Papagno:</bold> conceptualization, methodology, supervision, writing &#x02013; review and editing, investigation.</p></sec><sec id="ene70077-sec-0040"><title>Ethics Statement</title><p>The authors have nothing to report.</p></sec><sec sec-type="COI-statement" id="ene70077-sec-0041"><title>Conflicts of Interest</title><p>The authors declare no conflicts of interest.</p></sec><sec sec-type="supplementary-material"><title>Supporting information</title><supplementary-material id="ene70077-supitem-0001" position="float" content-type="local-data"><caption><p>
Data S1.
</p></caption><media xlink:href="ENE-32-e70077-s001.docx"/></supplementary-material></sec></body><back><sec sec-type="data-availability" id="ene70077-sec-0043"><title>Data Availability Statement</title><p>The data that support the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request.</p></sec><ref-list content-type="cited-references" id="ene70077-bibl-0001"><title>References</title><ref id="ene70077-bib-0001"><label>1</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal" id="ene70077-cit-0001">
